<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter Paul Rubens</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1577">1577</a>–<a href="/years/1640">1640</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter Paul Rubens was the leading painter of the Flemish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>, born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a> in Nassau, where his parents lived in exile. Trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, he ran a large workshop in Antwerp producing altarpieces, portraits and mythological scenes for the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and served as a diplomat for the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, negotiating peace between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1629–1630.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-paul-rubens.jpg" alt="Peter Paul Rubens" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Paul Rubens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Paul_Rubens_-_Self-portrait_-_RH.S.180_-_Rubenshuis_(after_restoration).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…sustained much of the art of the Low Countries: Rogier van der Weyden was city painter of Brussels, Dieric Bouts worked in Leuven, Pieter Bruegel the Elder lived in Antwerp and Brussels, and <b>Peter Paul Rubens</b> made Antwerp the centre of Flemish Baroque painting in the seventeenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The princely family’s art collection, one of the most important private collections in Europe, includes major works by <b>Peter Paul Rubens</b> and Frans Hals and is shown in the family’s Garden Palace and City Palace in Vienna; sales from it after the Second World War, when the family…</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-liechtenstein">Principality of Liechtenstein · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In music Giovanni Pierluigi da Palestrina became the model for Catholic composers; in art its painters and sculptors included El Greco, <b>Peter Paul Rubens</b> and Bernini.</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Siegen was the birthplace of <b>Peter Paul Rubens</b> in 1577, when his parents lived there in exile.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-nassau-siegen">Principality of Nassau-Siegen</a></li><li><blockquote>The provinces became a Counter-Reformation stronghold; <b>Rubens</b> worked in Antwerp.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-netherlands">Spanish Netherlands</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.88,8.02&amp;year=1617" aria-label="Siegen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4732 399 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Peter Paul Rubens"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5182 669h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5182 669h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.88,8.02&amp;year=1617">What was here in 1617? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
